  "architecture": "The two sides do different work in different places. The Ocelli folder viewer parses and decodes each file in a native process on this machine and sends 8-bit grey frames over loopback to the page, which draws them on a 2D canvas on the CPU. The cornerstone3D page parses and decodes in the browser, in dicom-image-loader's WebAssembly workers, and renders through WebGL. Memory and CPU are therefore counted over every process each side runs: for Ocelli the native process and every Chromium process, and for cornerstone3D every Chromium process, including the GPU process. This measures the local folder viewer example, not the Ocelli browser core, which has no viewer page to measure yet.",
  "not_shown": [
    "one machine and one browser build",
